{
  "concepts": {
    "hook": {
      "desc": "A Hook is a general term for a relationship between characters where one can get the other to do their will.\n\nThere are two types, Weak Hooks and Strong Hooks, which are acquired in different ways.\n\nHooks can be used to force through many interactions, for example Marriage proposals and Vassal Contract changes.",
      "name": "Hook"
    },
    "secret": {
      "desc": "Secrets are things that characters do not wish others to know because they are embarrassing, immoral or outright Criminal.\n\nSecrets can be discovered through the 'Find Secrets' Councillor Task for the Spymaster. Once you know someone's Secret, you can Blackmail the character to gain a Hook.",
      "name": "Secret"
    },
    "strong_hook": {
      "desc": "A Strong Hook is a very powerful Hook that you might have on a character who you have Blackmailed.\n\nStrong Hooks are much more powerful than Weak Hooks, and can force someone to do terrible deeds for you — such as to become your Agent in a Murder (in addition to everything a Weak Hook can do!)\n\nA Strong Hook also prevents the target from taking any hostile actions towards the holder.\n\nStrong Hooks are not spent when used, but instead go on a cooldown before they can be used again. The passive effect is not lost when the Hook is used.",
      "name": "Strong Hook"
    },
    "weak_hook": {
      "desc": "A Weak Hook is a fairly common Hook that you might have on a character who owes you a favor, or that you have manipulated to do your will.\n\nWhile there are many interactions where a Weak Hook can be used, for example in Marriage proposals, they cannot be used to force someone to become your Agent in a Murder and do not provide a passive benefit (for that, a Strong Hook is necessary.)\n\nEvery child born to a Noble House also owes the House Head an initial Weak Hook.\n\nOnce a Weak Hook is used, it is spent.",
      "name": "Weak Hook"
    }
  },
